        "reviewed_sha": "b5c38867a85f6c33df611d1a7e3dd369abb429c6",
        "evidence": "Supersedes my own warn of 2026-09-04. That entry said naming the two n=3 Jones values exposed a counter-fact (they coincide at q* ~ 0.7795446638) and treated the general-n per-summand character values as an upstream gap. The gap claim was WRONG: chi^lambda for general n and lambda is live in tools/hecke-engine/src/seminormal.rs (Hoefsmit) with MPFR variants and ~20 Python entry points via pyhecke_native 0.9.0. Measured with chi_lambdas_braid_mpfr at 50 dps: the characters separate the two summands on all 5 words tested AT q* itself, and the closed forms chi^[3](s1) = q, chi^[2,1](s1) = q - 1/q give difference 1/q, verified at 8 sample q to < 1e-45.",
        "notes": "So jones_channels_not_separating is a narrow negative about the pair those theorems named, not about the framework. Positive counterpart now Lean-proved beside it: `sigma_separates_n3_channels` — sigma_1 separates at every Descartes q, since q > 0 forces q^-1 != 0. Probe: computations/descartes_n3_channel_separation_probe.py + witness. Bean qou-qhs3. Still genuinely open, and it is formalisation not mathematics: constructing trSummand from the Hoefsmit values in Lean."
      }
